# Copy this file to .env and paste in YOUR OWN keys.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored and must stay that way. A key in a commit is a key on the
# internet — treat one that leaks as already spent, and revoke it in AI Studio.

# --- Gemini: the only key you need to start (session 1) -----------------------
# Create at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey — free, no card required.
# The SDK reads GEMINI_API_KEY automatically; you never pass it in code.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# ⚠️ FREE TIER PROMPTS ARE USED TO TRAIN GOOGLE'S MODELS.
# Google's pricing page lists "content used to improve our products" as Yes for
# the free tier, No for paid. So: never paste real customer data, real names,
# real card numbers or anything from your employer into a free-tier notebook.
# Every ticket in data/tickets.yml is invented for exactly this reason.

# --- What the free key does and doesn't unlock --------------------------------
# Free is enough for everything you are asked to build in this course: text,
# multimodal input (sending images/audio/PDFs), structured JSON output,
# streaming, and embeddings.
#
# Two capabilities need a BILLING-ENABLED key, and they appear only as
# instructor demos, marked 💳 in the notebooks:
#   · Google Search grounding   (S01 §7.4 — answering from the live web)
#   · Image generation          (S01 §7.5 — gemini-3.1-flash-image)
# Those cells are written to catch the error and explain it rather than crash,
# and every notebook ships with its outputs committed — so nothing in this
# course requires you to enable billing. If you do enable it, they just run.

# --- Other providers: from session 4, all optional ----------------------------
# From session 4 the assistant runs on LangChain, which names a model with one
# string, `provider:model` — so switching vendor is a line in this file rather
# than a rewrite. Session 4 demonstrates five providers. What they cost you:
#
#     google_genai   Gemini, via AI Studio      free key  ← you already have it
#     groq           open weights, hosted       free key  ← the second one, below
#     ollama         models on your own laptop  no key at all
#     openai         GPT                        billed from the first token
#     anthropic      Claude                     billed from the first token
#
# Gemini and Groq are the only two providers here you can run against a free
# hosted key, which is why those are the two the exercises use. The OpenAI and
# Anthropic paths are demonstrated from committed output — you are never asked
# to enable billing to finish a session.
#
# Note the UNDERSCORE in `google_genai`. The pip package is hyphenated
# (`langchain-google-genai`) and the provider id is not; the hyphenated form
# raises "Unable to infer model provider", which reads like a complaint about
# your model id and is not.

# --- Groq: a free key, and it is the fast one ---------------------------------
# Create at https://console.groq.com/keys — free, no card required.
# Groq serves OPEN-WEIGHTS models (Llama, Qwen, OpenAI's gpt-oss) on custom
# hardware, and the speed difference is the kind you notice without a stopwatch.
# It is worth having for exactly that reason: it makes "the provider is one line
# of configuration" something you can feel, not just read.
#
# The same free-tier caution as Gemini applies — assume prompts may be retained.
GROQ_API_KEY=

# To make Groq the assistant's provider, set BOTH lines (a Gemini model id means
# nothing to Groq), and note that the model name here says "openai" while the
# provider says "groq": the provider is who SERVES the model, not who made it.
#   SHOPWISE_PROVIDER=groq
#   SHOPWISE_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-20b

# --- Ollama: models on your own laptop, no key and no network -----------------
# The one provider with nothing to paste below, because there is nothing to
# paste. Ollama downloads open-weights models and runs them on your own machine:
# no key, no account, no bill, and no request ever leaves the laptop. That last
# point is the real lesson — it is the honest answer to "we cannot send customer
# data to a third party", and you should know it exists before someone tells you
# an LLM feature is impossible for compliance reasons.
#
#   1. install       https://ollama.com/download   (or: brew install ollama)
#   2. pull a model  ollama pull llama3.2:3b       (~2 GB, one time)
#   3. run the server  ollama serve                (leave it running)
#
# Then, with no key anywhere:
#   SHOPWISE_PROVIDER=ollama
#   SHOPWISE_MODEL=llama3.2:3b
#
# Expect it to be slower and weaker than the hosted models — a 3B model on a
# laptop is not gemini-3.1-flash-lite, and seeing exactly HOW it is worse is a
# more useful hour than reading a benchmark table. If your machine struggles,
# `llama3.2:1b` is half the size.

# --- Settings (all optional) --------------------------------------------------
# From session 3 the assistant's configuration is a typed Pydantic model —
# open the newest session-XX/project/config.py and the field list IS the
# documentation. Everything there has a working default, so this whole block can
# stay commented out. Every field is read from SHOPWISE_<FIELD>; the prefix keeps
# ShopWise's settings from colliding with the dozen other things on your machine
# that would also like a variable called MODEL.
#
# Print the resolved configuration at any time with:
#     cd session-04/project && uv run python config.py
#
#   SHOPWISE_PROVIDER=google_genai          # who serves the model (session 4 on)
#   SHOPWISE_MODEL=gemini-3.1-flash-lite    # course default: cheapest, fast
#   SHOPWISE_MODEL_STRONG=gemini-3.6-flash  # when quality matters more
#   SHOPWISE_MODEL_PRO=gemini-2.5-pro       # the free tier's best reasoning model
#   SHOPWISE_MODEL_IMAGE=gemini-3.1-flash-image  # image generation (billed only)
#   SHOPWISE_PROMPT_VERSION=v1              # the label stamped on every trace
#   SHOPWISE_USE_FEW_SHOT=false             # few-shot triage examples (measured: no gain)
#   SHOPWISE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=1024         # ceiling on one reply
#   SHOPWISE_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_S=60           # give up on one call
#   SHOPWISE_ENVIRONMENT=development        # development | staging | production
#
# S01 §9.6 has a cell that lists whatever model IDs your key can actually see
# today, which is the real source of truth when one is retired.

# --- LangSmith: from session 4 -----------------------------------------------
# Your own free account at https://smith.langchain.com — your traces stay in
# your workspace, nobody else's. Once tracing is on, every LLM call the
# assistant makes is recorded with its inputs, outputs, latency and token count,
# and you did not write a line of code to get it.
#
# These three keep their plain names — no SHOPWISE_ prefix — because the
# LangSmith SDK reads them out of the environment itself. Prefixing them would
# mean two variables per setting that have to agree.
#
# Leave TRACING false until the key below is filled in. Turned on without a key
# it warns (LangSmithMissingAPIKeyWarning) and then quietly posts traces nobody
# receives. Flipping this line to true is a step in session 4 — do it there and
# watch a run appear.
LANGSMITH_TRACING=false
LANGSMITH_API_KEY=
# The project is the folder your traces land in. "shopwise" is fine for the
# whole course because the workspace is yours alone. On a team this is the field
# that stops a load test burying someone's bug hunt: split by service, and never
# point staging at the same project as production.
LANGSMITH_PROJECT=shopwise

# --- Qdrant: session 8 runs locally, no signup and no keys ------------------
# Only fill these in at session 15, if you move to Qdrant Cloud.
QDRANT_URL=
QDRANT_API_KEY=
